 that each solve one part of the  total problem  the main tasks in this data to video  product are  importing and typing all the csv files  do the required amending to get the data  set in perfect shape  render all the video frames and then  encode all these frames into a single  video file  this project has two main phases where  the first is to import type and demand  the data the preparation phase  and the second phase is the creative  part where we use the importer data to  create the frames in the video file  this image illustrates most intermediate  files in the projects  input data enter at the top left moves  right and then down and then right again  until we reach the output video file  in between we can see the different jobs  and how many files they create  internally for example the frame  rendering job needs one plus one plus  seven input files to draw all the frames  these files in turn are generated from  the imported and typed data this is  stored as hundreds of thousands of files  out from the rendering job we have more  than 2800 files one for each frame in  the movie which are then video encoded  by ffmpeg into one output file  now why why do we see hundreds of  thousands of files in the csv import  jobs we will get back to that but  basically it is because we have 3 000  csv input files times 150 columns and we  want to be able to read the columns  independently for speed reasons  and in practice these numbers are  multiplied by n where n is the number of  cpus in the machine to take full  advantage of the parallel processing  hardware in a modern computer  nevertheless  these files are never visible to the  user we mentioned them here for  reference but we really don't need to  know this in order to write the program  this table shows the execution times for  the different parts of the project it  also shows how many iterations we did to  complete the movie  importing takes most of the time more  than half an hour and it creates  approximately 3 000 jobs and this is run  only once  amending the data set takes a few  iterations to get right but it takes  only six seconds per iteration  then we need to collect the data to  render and we do this using three  different jobs that executes in 44  seconds and it took 19 attempts to get  that one right  most of the development effort is then  spent in re in the rendering code here  we need to try out what looks good and  what does not  by looking in our work directory we  could see that we ran this job more than  100 times  and in order to watch the video we also  need to encode the frames into a video  file  encoding all frames takes 17 seconds  since ffmpeg can only use a few cpus  so we added a parameter that by default  only generates two seconds of video then  we're down to one second  so the total iteration time from  changing the source code to having a new  video to watch is only seven seconds  as we all know  fast iteration is fundamental for the  creative process being able to try out  ideas in only a few seconds is a game  changer  you may argue that this is fast because  we are using a fast computer  so we also run this on a laptop from  2018.  import them took 45 minutes and the  design iteration was less than 50  seconds  so this is still okay and if we just  render say the first 120 frames per  iteration until we get it right we are  down to maybe 10 seconds per iteration  so what i'm saying is that even an old  laptop can handle a 250 million row data  set with ease  now we move to another example  this time we use the new york city  yellow cab data sets where all taxi  trips in new york  are stored since 2019  this data set has 1.6 billion rows  and to show that it's easy to work with  data sets of this size on a single  computer we've created an animation  based on this data too  this data set is a relatively high  quality but there are a lot of outliers  and a few other issues for example if we  create a heat map of the drop-off  locations for one of the vendors we get  the plot to the top right here we can  see that it looks rather noisy there are  drop-offs all over the place including  the hudson river  and even more worrying is that if we  swap the longitude and the latitude  coordinates a mirror of new york city  will manifest itself somewhere close to  antarctica so some cleanup is required  the data set contains a lot of  information here we have created a  minute by minute representation of the  number of active taxi trips  we can for example zoom in on the  january 2016 united states blizzard and  study what happened then in great detail  the video  the example video that we will show  however  uh it shows the impact of the corona  pandemic to the new york city taxi  business and i will start the video now  you can see two identical maps of new  york city to the left we have 2019 and  to the right we have 2020  and the amount of taxi drop-offs are  visualized in green  now as the number of confirmed cases of  covet 19 in new york city here in blue  increases  we can see that the number of taxi trips  in orange starts to decrease  this video is running at 60 frames per  second where each frame represents one  hour new york time  and this video is also available on  youtube and you can also find the source  code that generates it on our github  page  importing the 1.6 billion rows took less  than 11 minutes  we don't need all this data for the  video but since importing is so fast  there is little point in importing less  then we need to download some map tiles  from openstreetmap  data about covet cases etc and this  takes seven seconds  most of this time is actually spent  waiting in the openstreetmap api  the design iteration then is 12 seconds  for this project and added 26 seconds to  that to encode the full video file  before uploading it to youtube  in addition to what has been already  mentioned xxx has more features that are  required or really helpful when running  projects in production in this talk  we've only got time to talk about one of  them the high performance access data  xx stores tableau data using something  called a dataset is an efficient  representation designed for high-speed  parallel streaming access  the dataset is an extension of the job  we mentioned  data sets could be linked to each other  vertically or horizontally to add more  rows and columns in constant time  and data sets are strictly typed but  there are many types to choose from  including python pixel meaning that we  can include almost anything in a data  set  here is an illustration of how the data  is stored  imagine the taxi data set with columns  such as latitude longitude and timestamp  each column is stored as separate files  so that we do not need to go through all  of the data in order to read just a  single column  i would say reading one or a few columns  is by far the most common case  furthermore each column is split into  separate files or slices so that the  different cpu cores in the machine can  read one file each in parallel  ericsson is an example illustrating how  efficient the data set operations are  imagine that we want to amend the model  column in the back place data set  what we do then is that we read the  model column one slice per cpu in  parallel we do the required modification  on one all the cpu cores and then we  write back the new column one file per  cpu or slice this is a perfectly  parallel operation  and it requires very little memory since  data is streamed through the program  and below here we can see an example  what the code might look like we read  the model data from an iterator and we  just write the modified data to a data  set writer and this for loop is then run  in parallel on all cpus  and the modification we did was  replacing a double space by a single  space in this case  note however that we never modify data  instead we create new columns keeping  the original ones for reference and  reproducibility  now parallel processing works best if  data can be partitioned into independent  sets  look at this example of a data set with  three slices our task is to compute how  many fruits there are of each kind  since for example apple exists in all  three slices  we need to spend time and memory  computing it counting it in every slice  so we can only compute a partial result  in each slice and then we need to merge  all these partial results to get our  outputs  this is more or less how mapreduce works  it's both slow and memory intensive and  it can be done in a better way  the exact solution is to re-partition  the data based on a hash function  we move each fruit to the slice we get  from applying a hash function to the  fruits in this example for we have  apples and bananas hashing to the same  slice and they are marked with red so  all apples and bananas go to slice zero  kiwis and pears go to slice one and so  on and so forth  now each fruits will then only exist in  one slice and we can count them  independently processing is perfectly  parallel and there is no need to merge  anything anymore  in addition hash partitioning is a very  fast operation and is run only once the  amortized cost approaches zero as you  reuse the result  and again data is never modified the  hash partition data is stored in a new  data set so it's just another view of  the same data yeah so conclusion is that  we can have faster development time  fewer mistakes full transparency and we  can work with data sets billions of rows  in a few minutes on a single machine  and here i provide references to the  blog manual and all the source code that  was mentioned in in this report thank  you  thank you so much um so we have a few  questions so from alex is this a desk  alternative if so how does it  compare in design and performance  oh that's a very good question  this runs on a single machine so you  don't need to bother to set up a cluster  in order to run it and you can run it on  a laptop or you can run it on a rack  server with with a hundred cpus if you  like  so i would would say it's it's kind of  different  okay we have another question from uh  maxim how fast would your program run if  it used pandas for importing dataset and  pi arrow.parkhead  uh it's it's pretty different we we use  a different way a very efficient  low-level way of storing things and park  yet use another one so they're not  really compatible of course you can  transform from one to the other if you  would like to  uh but we don't have any lock in so you  can actually get the data out again into  csv file if you like  so i would say you don't need to use  formats like pocket to use this you can  just use csv files  okay great so um our time is over um if  you have any more discussion that you  would want to have with the speakers  please move it to slack or gather town  thank you so much to our speakers dr  brickman and carl for your talk and  being part of the pi data global 2021.  thank you
 

